Source
World Health Organization — Medication Without Harm
Again, this is an authoritative WHO source rather than a research paper.
What it addresses
The initiative focuses on reducing avoidable harm associated with medication use.
Important areas include:
Medication errors
Prescribing
Dispensing
Administration
Patient involvement
Communication
Medication safety across healthcare transitions
WHO has estimated the global cost associated with medication errors at approximately US$42 billion annually.
CAREGRID future connection
This supports the future extension:
RFID Patient ID
      ↓
Secure Patient Record
      ↓
Prescribed Medication
      ↓
Dose
      ↓
Scheduled Time
      ↓
Nurse Dashboard Reminder
Safety boundary
CAREGRID should not:
Prescribe medication
Change the prescribed dose
Decide treatment
Replace the doctor
Instead:
CAREGRID displays authorized medication information and provides workflow reminders.
Design decision
The RFID and medication features should therefore be described as future care-coordination functionality, not as autonomous medication management